Assistant 2's response is more accurate in describing the outcome of a decrease in the satellite's speed. When the speed of a satellite in a circular orbit decreases, the centripetal force (which is provided by gravity in the case of orbital motion) decreases, leading to a decrease in the orbital radius. This is because the gravitational force, being inversely proportional to the square of the distance, increases as the satellite moves closer to the Earth, thereby increasing the centripetal acceleration and allowing the satellite to maintain a circular path at a lower altitude. The period of revolution, however, does not remain the same; according to Kepler's third law, as the orbital radius decreases, the period of revolution also decreases due to the increased gravitational force and consequent increase in orbital speed at lower altitudes.

